# THE COUNTEREXAMPLE THAT MAKES THE DIRECTORY RULE NEED A DYNSYM CHECK.
#
# `/usr/include/net/if.h` is a glibc header -- its symbols are in libc. But
# `libnet` is an unrelated packet-crafting library that occupies the name, and
# on this box it is INSTALLED. So a directory-derived guess passes the
# does-this-library-exist guard, links, loads, and dies on `undefined symbol`
# in a library the user never named: a QUIET failure where there was a loud
# one, which is a regression even though the directory rule is right for every
# other directory here.
#
# The fix is not to special-case the name. libnet.so.9 exports NONE of this
# header's symbols, so requiring the candidate to export the symbol being
# imported refuses it on evidence, and the symbol is then attributed to the
# library that really defines it. The Makefile row asserts the binary carries
# no DT_NEEDED on libnet.so.9 -- and establishes that libnet.so.9 is installed
# FIRST, because on a box without it this row would pass by absence and certify
# nothing.
#
# 1 is cross-checked against the kernel by the Makefile row, which compares it
# with `ip link`'s own index for lo rather than trusting a constant.
import "/usr/include/net/if.h"

print(if_nametoindex("lo"))
